\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pathfinder-note}

\title{Coalition Context in Pairwise Credit Assignment}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{The pair}

Q, \emph{Competitive Market Behavior of LLMs}, evaluates LLM traders in a continuous double auction, finding incomplete equilibrium convergence and heterogeneous trading behavior. P, \emph{MARS-RA}, uses multimodal pairwise judgments, Bradley--Terry aggregation, and potential-based reward shaping to support cooperative multi-agent learning with changing active membership.

\section{The connexion pursued}

The connexion concerns whether pairwise judgments recover
coalition-dependent contribution, rather than merely a consistent preference
among agents. Q supplies a market structure with exactly calculable gains
from trade; P supplies an aggregation mechanism whose statistical target is
the judge's latent preference. P's Shapley interpretation additionally
assumes that this preference already represents Shapley contribution.
The proposed audit tests that semantic premise. \ledger{6, 8, 9}

This direction superseded an investigation of membership-dependent
shaping boundaries. A possible connexion through urgency language was not
established: Q studies traders, not contribution judges, and its reasoning
analysis does not establish causal mechanisms. See
\url{ada/research_note.md}, lines 3--13 and 102--113.

The earlier conditional results are retained below. The first extension
responded to the verifier's request for classical attribution and evidence
beyond a selected reversal. The extension beginning in
Section~\ref{sec:actual-pipeline} addresses the objection that the
static-score bound did not cover P's per-state refitting pipeline.
It establishes a sharp small-team obstruction but also shows
that refitting recovers most rankings on the audited reachable market
families. These conclusions must be read together.
\ledger{11, 21, 25, 26, 29}

The extension beginning in Section~\ref{sec:family-extension}
responds to review 31. It adds the exact
dropout-family error formula, constructive context repairs, social-choice
attribution, and a comparator shared across all reachable active counts.
It preserves the correction in entry 38.
The empirical material remains thin, and novelty of the exact family
formula has not been established. \ledger{31, 34, 36, 38, 39}

The appended Section~\ref{sec:general-extension} responds to the latest
review, entry 41. It retains the preceding results and adds an
arbitrary-game projection on the linear win-score scale, a constructive
local-order result, and a participation-model lower bound.
These additions do not supply empirical judge measurements or establish
publication readiness. \ledger{41, 43, 46, 47, 50}

\section{An exact contribution reversal}

Choose an explicit cooperative evaluation game on traders:
\[
W(C)=
\max_{\substack{M\text{ a matching}\\\text{within }C}}
\sum_{(b,s)\in M}(v_b-c_s).
\]
The empty matching is permitted. This is maximum feasible surplus,
not realized individual profit or a trajectory-level causal contribution.
It is an evaluation target imposed for the audit, not an assertion that
Q's competitive agents pursue a cooperative objective.
It is a homogeneous-good special case of the Shapley--Shubik assignment
game. \ledger{3, 6, 9, 17}

For identical buyers with value \(3\) and identical sellers with cost \(1\),
\[
W(C)=2\min\{b(C),s(C)\},
\]
where \(b(C)\) and \(s(C)\) count buyers and sellers.
This is the classical glove market, scaled by two, rather than a new
game-theoretic construction. Hold the local features of a focal buyer
\(B_1\) and seller \(S_1\) fixed and compare
\[
I_A=\{B_1,B_2,S_1\},
\qquad
I_B=\{B_1,S_1,S_2\}.
\]
Both coalitions have three members and surplus \(2\). Their Shapley
credits are
\[
\begin{aligned}
(\varphi_{B_1},\varphi_{B_2},\varphi_{S_1})(I_A)
   &= (1/3,1/3,4/3),\\
(\varphi_{B_1},\varphi_{S_1},\varphi_{S_2})(I_B)
   &= (4/3,1/3,1/3).
\end{aligned}
\]
In \(I_A\), the sole seller adds surplus \(2\) whenever at least one
buyer precedes it in a uniformly random ordering, an event of probability
\(2/3\). A particular buyer adds \(2\) only when the
seller precedes it and the other buyer follows it, with probability
\(1/6\). Interchanging roles gives \(I_B\). Thus the focal
buyer-minus-seller credit gap reverses from \(-1\) to \(+1\).
\ledger{6, 11, 17}

The derivation and exact permutation checks are recorded in
\url{ada/research_note.md}, lines 15--49 and 115--118;
the original numerical record is
\url{ada/run_002_coalition_credit.log}.

\section{Why consistent aggregation can miss the reversal}

\begin{claim}
Under the controlled pair-local, role-exchangeable comparison model
below, population Bradley--Terry aggregation cannot recover both focal
Shapley orderings, even when every pair is compared.
\end{claim}

Assume each query contains only the compared agents' local features,
neutral labels, and fixed task text. The inputs do not reveal the
additional teammate. Impose exchangeability within each role and
symmetrize presentation order. Let a buyer beat a seller with probability
\(q\), where \(0<q<1\); same-role comparisons have expected score \(1/2\).
These are stress-test assumptions, not observations about P's actual
prompts. \ledger{6, 8}

All these comparisons have an exact Bradley--Terry representation:
\[
c_B=d,\qquad c_S=0,\qquad
d=\log\frac{q}{1-q}.
\]
For each edge, these scores reproduce its comparison probability.
On the complete graph with interior probabilities, the population
likelihood identifies the score differences uniquely. Centering the
scores removes the translation ambiguity. Consequently,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widehat c_{B_1}(I_A)-\widehat c_{S_1}(I_A)&=d,\\
\widehat c_{B_1}(I_B)-\widehat c_{S_1}(I_B)&=d.
\end{aligned}
\]
Softmax preserves the ordering. No fixed \(d\) matches both strict
oracle rankings. If score gaps are evaluated on the stipulated Shapley
scale, their mean squared error across the two contexts is
\[
\frac{(d+1)^2+(d-1)^2}{2}=d^2+1\geq 1.
\]
Additional comparisons can improve estimation of the fixed preference
without making it a contribution preference. \ledger{6, 8, 9}

See \url{ada/research_note.md}, lines 51--80.
The recorded checks verify exact probability fits and zero likelihood
gradients at \(q\in\{0.25,0.5,0.75\}\).
This is an analytic construction, not an LLM experiment.
Population preferences can be perfectly consistent while the contribution
ranking is wrong; no zero empirical cycle rate is claimed.

\section{Objections and scope corrections}

\begin{objection}
The asymmetric coalitions are not states demonstrated in Q's experiment.
\end{objection}

Q starts with balanced buyer and seller counts, and execution removes
one of each. The duplicate-role triplets are therefore a controlled
extension, not a reported Q trajectory. \ledger{7}

Balanced examples with distinct values on Q's price grid also reverse
the focal ranking. Keeping focal buyer value \(3\) and seller cost \(1\),
both following markets have surplus \(2\):
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{Buyer values}&\text{Seller costs}&
\varphi_{B_1}-\varphi_{S_1}\\ \hline
(3,\,2.75)&(1,\,3.25)&-7/8\\
(3,\,0.75)&(1,\,1.25)&+7/8
\end{array}
\]
These establish semantic reversal despite equal headcounts. They do
not establish the all-pairs obstruction for arbitrary inconsistent
comparison rules: comparisons involving other traders could change the
fitted focal gap. That stronger conclusion requires a
context-independent scalar Bradley--Terry comparator.
See \url{ada/research_note.md}, lines 82--88. \ledger{7}

\begin{objection}
The whole comparison graph can reveal coalition composition.
\end{objection}

Correct. A coalition-aware aggregator could exploit role multiplicities.
The result concerns standard Bradley--Terry fitting when the local
probabilities already admit an exact fit. Bradley--Terry does not require
scores to remain fixed over time: P estimates a new vector at each step.
The invariance above follows from the constructed input distributions,
not a general restriction on contextual ranking. \ledger{8, 17}

\begin{objection}
The construction refutes P's Shapley interpretation or its policy
invariance claim.
\end{objection}

Neither follows. P's Shapley proposition assumes the semantic agreement
that this audit investigates. A poorly aligned potential also need not
change optimal policies when potential-based shaping retains the
required boundary terms. Its effects on finite-budget learning remain
unmeasured. \ledger{2, 9, 19}

A separate transfer-invariance audit remains available: changing
transaction price changes individual rents but not a fixed match's
surplus \(v-c\); the two-player surplus game assigns each party
\((v-c)/2\). This could distinguish rent capture from contribution,
but no judge experiment tested it. \ledger{3}

\section{Secondary finding: membership boundaries}

P's active-agent score vector needs a fixed-identity interpretation
before potentials at successive times can be subtracted. Embed each
agent's potential as zero while inactive and at the global terminal
state. Membership-dependent normalization alone then does not prevent
telescoping. \ledger{1, 2}

For one agent, write
\(F_t=\gamma\Phi_{t+1}-\Phi_t\), let \(m_t\) be the retained shaping
weight, and assume \(\Phi_T=0\). Reindexing gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^t m_tF_t
={}&-m_0\Phi_0\\
&+\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}
\gamma^t(m_{t-1}-m_t)\Phi_t.
\end{aligned}
\]
With all weights equal to one, only the initial term remains.
Changing masks can introduce intermediate boundary terms.
Under zero inactive potentials, pre-transition activity masks preserve
absorbing exits but omit entry credits on respawn; post-transition
masks can omit exit debits. \ledger{4}

The four-agent equal-score example has total shaping return
\(-1\) whether agents trade or wait. Omitting only the departing agents'
exit-transition shaping changes the trade return to \(-1/2\), while
global terminal terms remain included. The identity was checked on
\(2{,}048\) rational cases. See
\url{ada/research_note.md}, lines 102--118, and the recorded checks in
\url{ada/run_001_membership_shaping.log}. \ledger{4}

This is a conditional implementation audit. P's actual return masks and
bootstrapping were not inspected, and a loss mask alone does not imply
reward masking. The boundary mechanism is established literature,
so it was not retained as the main novelty claim. \ledger{5, 9}

\section{The initial audit proposal}

The first round proposed frozen-state comparisons using the two triplets,
holding the judge, focal observations, and Bradley--Terry fitter fixed.
The information arms were pair-only inputs, pair inputs plus active-agent
count, and pair inputs plus coalition capabilities. Both triplets have
three agents, so count-only information cannot identify changed scarcity.
Aliases and presentation order would be randomized, with repetitions
varied independently of context and both fixed-query and matched-token
budgets reported. \ledger{9}

The primary outcome was recovery of the focal ordering reversal.
Extra repetitions improving consistency without recovering the reversal,
followed by recovery with capability context, would support the diagnosis.
Finding that P's actual inputs already reveal the needed context would
limit its applicability. This remains a valid control experiment;
the next round added a broader structural test before any judge calls.
See \url{ada/research_note.md}, lines 90--100. \ledger{11, 19}

\section{Extension after ITERATE: Q's actual schedule}

The verifier judged the original conditional example insufficient for
paper review. The extension therefore enumerated all \(705{,}431\)
nonempty balanced subsets of Q's actual eleven-by-eleven reservation grid,
with separate filters for removals through nonnegative-surplus and
strictly positive-surplus trades. These are possible active sets,
not measured frequencies in Q's simulations. \ledger{11, 13}

\subsection{Exact credits and reachability}

For an active set \(I\), restrict the previously defined game to
coalitions \(C\subseteq I\). At price threshold \(p\), write
\[
\begin{aligned}
B_C(p)&=|\{b\in C:v_b>p\}|,\\
S_C(p)&=|\{s\in C:c_s<p\}|.
\end{aligned}
\]
Then
\[
W_I(C)=\int_{0.75}^{3.25}
       \min\{B_C(p),S_C(p)\}\,dp.
\]
To verify this, sort buyer values descending and seller costs ascending.
The optimal surplus sums positive differences between corresponding
entries. At each threshold, the number of these intervals containing
\(p\) is the smaller eligible count.

Each integrand is a glove game, with ineligible traders as dummies.
For \(a\) eligible buyers and \(b\) eligible sellers, an eligible buyer's
unit-surplus Shapley credit is
\[
g_B(a,b)=
\sum_{k=0}^{a-1}\sum_{\ell=k+1}^{b}
\frac{\binom{a-1}{k}\binom{b}{\ell}}
     {(a+b)\binom{a+b-1}{k+\ell}}.
\]
The buyer contributes one precisely when more sellers than other buyers
precede it in a random ordering. Also \(g_S(a,b)=g_B(b,a)\).
Shapley linearity reduces the grid calculation to ten intervals of width
\(1/4\). This is a computational use of classical glove games.
See \url{ada/iterate_11_extension.md}, lines 11--46. \ledger{13, 17}

For removed buyer and seller lists sorted in increasing order, strict
profitable-removal reachability is equivalent to
\[
v_{R_B,k}>c_{R_S,k}\qquad\text{for every }k.
\]
The weak inequality gives the nonnegative-surplus version.
The ordered matching condition is necessary; matching corresponding
entries supplies sufficiency. Each intended pair can trade at a price
between its cost and value by posting an ask and then a crossing bid.
This establishes mechanism reachability within Q's iteration cap,
not that its prompted agents choose these sequences.
See \url{ada/iterate_11_extension.md}, lines 48--58.
\ledger{13, 18}

\subsection{Best context-free scores}

Assign an arbitrary real score \(x_i\) to each trader identity,
generously refitting the scores separately for each active count \(m\).
Such scores can depend on role and reservation price, but cannot change
when only the remaining teammates change. For the reachable family
\(\mathcal F_m\), define
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Delta_{ij}(I)&=\varphi_i(I)-\varphi_j(I),\\
E_m&=\min_x
\frac{\displaystyle
 \sum_{I\in\mathcal F_m}\sum_{\{i,j\}\subset I}
 \bigl(x_i-x_j-\Delta_{ij}(I)\bigr)^2}
 {|\mathcal F_m|\binom{2m}{2}}.
\end{aligned}
\]
States and pairs within states receive uniform weight.
Using gaps removes the common-offset ambiguity.
The normal equations form a weighted co-occurrence graph Laplacian;
the peer computation solved them in rational arithmetic after fixing
a gauge. See \url{ada/iterate_11_extension.md}, lines 60--85.
\ledger{13}

After five strictly profitable removals, \(19{,}404\) active sets have
six buyers and six sellers. The exact optimum gives
\[
\sqrt{E_6}=0.1528465548,
\]
in reservation-price currency units, leaving \(11.6096\%\) of squared
oracle pair gaps unexplained. This is an optimal approximation bound,
not a fitted judge's performance.
See \url{ada/run_006_q_schedule.log}, line 7. \ledger{13}

A separate optimistic ranking bound lets each buyer--seller identity
pair choose its fixed direction independently. If that pair has
\(N_+\) positive and \(N_-\) negative oracle gaps across states,
it must make at least \(\min(N_+,N_-)\) errors.
Summing gives \(59{,}882\) errors among \(622{,}936\) strict comparisons,
or \(9.6129\%\). A coherent scalar ranking cannot improve this relaxed
bound; attainment by a scalar ranking was not established.
Oracle ties are excluded, with predicted ties scored as half-errors.
\ledger{13}

The effect is concentrated near ties. Recomputing the bound after
filtering by oracle gap gives
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}
\text{Minimum gap}&\text{Comparisons}&\text{Errors}&
 \text{Rate}\\ \hline
\text{Any strict gap}&622936&59882&9.61\%\\
0.10&415820&23739&5.71\%\\
0.25&301946&9259&3.07\%\\
0.50&176258&1617&0.917\%
\end{array}
\]
See \url{ada/run_009_q_margins.log}, line 2.
The structural loss is measurable, but these numbers do not establish
that context-free ranking is generally ineffective. \ledger{15}

\subsection{A reachable reversal and cases without ranking loss}

Let reservation index \(k\) denote \(0.75+0.25k\).
Consider active index sets
\[
\begin{aligned}
B&=\{0,1,2,3,4,5\},\\
S&=\{0,1,2,4,5,10\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Remove buyer \(1\) and seller \(0\) through a strictly profitable trade.
For the unchanged focal buyer \(4\) and seller \(2\), the Shapley gap
changes from
\[
\varphi_{B_4}-\varphi_{S_2}=\frac{2}{15}
\quad\text{to}\quad
\varphi_{B_4}-\varphi_{S_2}=-\frac{9}{80}.
\]
Both states are reachable from Q's initial market through strictly
profitable removals. This supplies a reversal along a feasible sequence.
Keeping focal cards unchanged is an audit intervention; Q's actual
public history changes. See
\url{ada/iterate_11_extension.md}, lines 105--115. \ledger{15}

The negative cases matter. A final buyer--seller pair has equal Shapley
credits, so constant scores represent its gap exactly.
Reflection-symmetric active markets admit fixed intrinsic-strength
rankings without strictly reversed comparisons, although exact ties
among dummy traders require care.
The \(55\) four-trader states reachable after nine strictly profitable
removals have no strict cross-role reversals. Thus the strongest
actual-grid result concerns larger residual teams, not direct evidence
against P's two-to-four-agent embodied tasks. \ledger{18}

More generally, exact context-free gap representation requires pair
gaps to agree across their observed contexts and sum to zero around
cycles of the co-occurrence graph. Fixed strict rankings require the
union of required strict inequalities to be acyclic, after contracting
any equalities required to hold exactly.
See \url{ada/iterate_11_extension.md}, lines 117--125.

\begin{objection}
The exhaustive bound establishes a limitation of every pair-local
implementation of P.
\end{objection}

It does not. An inconsistent local comparator can produce different
population BT fits on different active subsets. P refits at each step.
The exhaustive bound measures the inadequacy of a context-free latent
target, whereas the original all-edges obstruction applies only to the
controlled, exactly BT-consistent comparator.
A capability histogram is sufficient context for this market oracle;
whether real judges use it remains untested. \ledger{17, 19}

The threshold formula was checked against independent subset
enumeration on \(263\) small markets. Further checks covered eight
twelve-agent markets, the nested reversal, and \(100\) exhaustive
small matching tests. These are recorded computational checks,
not replications of Q or P. \ledger{13, 18}

\section{Extension: finite-query BT existence}

P specifies one query per ordered pair, fractional tie counts,
an unregularized likelihood objective, and softmax of its maximizer.
Its proposition assumes a connected comparison graph, while its proof
sketch invokes regularized MLE without specifying a regularizer in the
algorithm. See \url{ada/iterate_11_extension.md}, lines 127--161.
\ledger{16}

For a finite, unique unregularized BT estimate up to translation,
the directed positive-win graph must be strongly connected.
Connectedness of the undirected queried design is insufficient.
This is the classical Ford condition, not a new result. \ledger{16, 17}

With two agents and two independent, tie-free queries, let \(p\) be
the probability of preferring the same focal agent after accounting for
presentation order. The estimate is finite only if the decisions split:
\[
\Pr(\text{no finite MLE})=p^2+(1-p)^2\geq\frac12.
\]
With \(r\) independent queries per order and independent tie probability
\(\tau\), where \(p\) is conditional on a non-tie, this becomes
\[
(1-\tau)^{2r}
\bigl[p^{2r}+(1-p)^{2r}\bigr].
\]

Exhaustive support-graph enumeration gives the following probabilities
with one independent, tie-free query per order:
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r}
\text{Agents}&\text{Equal scores}&
 \text{Adjacent preference }0.9\\ \hline
2&50.000\%&82.000\%\\
3&28.125\%&94.093\%\\
4&11.914\%&98.132\%
\end{array}
\]
The last column uses equally spaced log-scores, so nonadjacent
preferences are more decisive. These are analytic sampling probabilities,
not observed LLM failure rates. See
\url{ada/run_007_bt_existence.log}, lines 1--11.
Direct enumeration of binary samples independently checked the results.
\ledger{16, 18}

For the complete comparison design, a non-strongly-connected outcome
graph has ordered strongly connected components. Along the extended
likelihood limit, softmax mass concentrates on the top component.
For two agents with unanimous outcomes, the limit is one-hot.
Numerical stopping rules, clipping, or a prior can therefore affect
reported potentials. Ties and dependencies change the probabilities
above; no actual fitter failure in P was observed. \ledger{16}

If P's connectivity premise means undirected design connectivity,
the two-agent equal-strength example also exceeds its permitted
failure probability: nonexistence has probability \(1/2\), whereas
\(n^{-2}=1/4\). If strong connectivity of the realized directed graph
was intended, these samples violate that premise instead; the issue is
then an unstated and unensured condition.
A specified regularized or bounded estimator and appropriate assumptions
would require a revised analysis. This does not refute P's conditional
Shapley interpretation or its reported learning curves.
\ledger{17}

\section{Outside sources and novelty limits}

The earlier account used the following sources, retrieved through
the shared pool and read in relevant slices, as recorded in
\url{ada/research_note.md}, lines 115--124:
\begin{itemize}
\item Devlin and Kudenko (2012), on dynamic-potential invariance:
\url{https://www.ifaamas.org/Proceedings/aamas2012/papers/2C_3.pdf}.
\ledger{5}
\item Grzes (2017), on episodic shaping boundaries:
\url{https://kar.kent.ac.uk/60614/}. \ledger{5}
\item Li et al., \emph{Shapley Counterfactual Credits for Multi-Agent
Reinforcement Learning}:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.00285}.
The recorded pooled-LaTeX reading, lines 187--198 and 297--328,
establishes existing coalition-aware attribution through a centralized
critic and counterfactual marginal contributions. \ledger{9}
\end{itemize}

The first ITERATE round corrected the missing classical attribution and
expanded the novelty check:
\begin{itemize}
\item Shapley and Shubik's glove-market antecedent (1969):
\url{https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(69)90008-8};
their assignment game (1971):
\url{https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01753437}.
Repeated pool requests for the latter's full text were rate-limited.
Its abstract was available, but its full proof was not read.
The calculations here are explicit independent derivations.
\ledger{11, 17}
\item Seshadri, Peysakhovich, and Ugander (2019),
\emph{Discovering Context Effects from Raw Choice Data}:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.03266}.
Recorded pooled-LaTeX reading at lines 145--165, 180--258, and
303--334 establishes contextual choice modeling as prior work.
\ledger{17}
\item Newman (2023),
\emph{Efficient Computation of Rankings from Pairwise Comparisons}:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.00076}.
Recorded pooled-LaTeX reading at lines 119--126 and 242--269
covers nonexistence and priors. Ford's original criterion is associated
with \url{https://doi.org/10.1080/00029890.1957.11989117}.
The calculated sparse-query probabilities are not a new existence
theorem. \ledger{16, 17}
\end{itemize}

The extension also records an abstract-level check of Bower and Balzano,
\emph{Preference Modeling with Context-Dependent Salient Features},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.09615}.
Its flattened source retained unresolved subfile commands; technical
details were not verified and no PDF extraction was used.
See \url{ada/iterate_11_extension.md}, lines 163--169.

Neither context sensitivity, coalition-aware attribution, nor the
glove-game reversal is new. The prospective contribution is a controlled,
oracle-grounded benchmark and intervention study. The novelty review
was not exhaustive. \ledger{17, 19}

\section{Open questions and next step at the first extension}

The empirical material remains thin. No real judge calls, MARL training,
implementation audit of P, or replication of Q were performed.
The peer environment had no configured inference credentials or exposed
model-inference endpoint; this limitation was recorded without printing
secrets in \url{ada/run_004_environment.log}, line 2.
The numerical evidence is an exact structural audit under an explicit
state distribution, not evidence about how frequently real agents
encounter the problem. \ledger{14, 19}

Open questions were whether P's actual images and prompts already reveal
coalition capabilities, whether its estimator uses regularization,
whether real judges approximate a context-free target, and how Q's
trajectory distribution weights the problematic states.
Transfer to small embodied teams and effects on finite-budget learning
remained untested. The margin analysis left open whether the observed
structural disagreement has substantial practical consequences.
\ledger{15, 18, 19}

The proposed next step was the frozen-state judge audit, using
reachable actual-grid states as the primary test and the classical
triplets as controls. Hold the judge, rendering, and explicitly
prior-controlled BT fitter fixed. Compare pair-only, pair-plus-count,
and pair-plus-capability-histogram inputs. Randomize labels and order;
measure exchangeability rather than assuming it. Vary repetition
independently of information, reporting fixed-query and matched-token
budgets. \ledger{19}

Measure raw comparison probabilities as well as fitted scores, since
aggregation itself can introduce context dependence.
Report margin-stratified oracle ranking errors, score-gap errors,
directed-graph separation frequencies, and potential entropy.
These measurements separate semantic error from finite-query estimator
behavior. See \url{ada/iterate_11_extension.md}, lines 173--181.

A capability-context improvement that survives these controls would
support the proposed intervention. Finding that existing observations
or contextual aggregation already recover the oracle would limit the
diagnosis. If disagreements remain near ties without learning impact,
the claim should shrink. The following extension changes which fixture
should be tested first, while retaining these controls.
\ledger{19, 29}

\section{Extension after ITERATE 21: the actual refitting pipeline}
\label{sec:actual-pipeline}

The verifier accepted the previous exact audit under its
conditions but judged it insufficient for paper review. Its central
objection was substantive: a fixed scalar score is not the class P uses,
because P refits BT on each active set. The requested extension therefore
allowed arbitrary identity-specific pair probabilities and retained
per-state population BT fitting. It also asked for small-team results,
certificates, cardinal-error analysis, and further literature checks.
No real-judge experiment was requested again without inference access.
\ledger{21, 22}

This extension removes the earlier exchangeability and scalar-consistency
assumptions. It still freezes the pair-visible inputs: changing unseen
teammates must not change a pair's outcome distribution. P's actual images
or task text may violate that restriction by revealing teammates.
Thus the result covers a specified comparison-and-refit class, not every
possible implementation of P.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 9--21.
\ledger{23, 29}

\subsection{Population BT order is win-score order}

After equally weighting the two presentation orders and assigning half a
win to ties, write the effective probabilities as
\[
p_{ij}+p_{ji}=1,
\qquad
a_{ij}=p_{ij}-\frac12=-a_{ji}.
\]
Allow every pair its own probability, with no assumed transitivity.
Assume interior probabilities so the population estimate is finite.
For active set \(I\), define centered expected wins
\[
r_i(I)=\sum_{j\in I\setminus\{i\}}a_{ij}.
\]
With \(\sigma(z)=(1+e^{-z})^{-1}\), population BT stationarity gives
\[
\sum_{j\in I\setminus\{i\}}\sigma(c_i-c_j)
=
\sum_{j\in I\setminus\{i\}}p_{ij}.
\]

Subtract the equations for \(i\) and \(j\). If \(c_i>c_j\), the mutual
comparison contributes positively, and every common-opponent term
\(\sigma(c_i-c_k)-\sigma(c_j-c_k)\) is positive.
Equality or reversal of the fitted scores gives equality or reversal of
the win-score difference. Hence
\[
\operatorname{sign}(c_i-c_j)
=
\operatorname{sign}\bigl(r_i(I)-r_j(I)\bigr),
\]
where
\[
r_i(I)-r_j(I)
=
2a_{ij}
+\sum_{k\in I\setminus\{i,j\}}(a_{ik}-a_{jk}).
\]
The active comparison graph can therefore supply context even when
each edge probability is fixed. The BT/Borda order equivalence is
established prior work, not a new ranking theorem.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 23--52.
\ledger{23, 29}

For a finite family of required strict oracle orders, collect the signed
integer coefficient rows into a matrix \(A\), and the independent entries
of \(a\) into \(x\). Exact rank representation is equivalent to
\(Ax>0\). By homogeneity, this is equivalent to \(Ax\geq\mathbf1\);
a finite feasible direction can subsequently be scaled to give interior
probabilities. Infeasibility is certified by
\[
\lambda\geq0,\qquad
\lambda\ne0,\qquad
A^\mathsf{T}\lambda=0.
\]
These rational certificates make the obstruction independently checkable.
They do not require treating a numerical solver's failure as a proof.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, line 54.
\ledger{23, 27}

\subsection{A sharp small-team obstruction}

\begin{claim}
Take two buyers and two sellers, with every buyer able to trade
profitably with every seller. Across the four active triples obtained
by deleting one trader, any fixed reciprocal pair comparator followed
by population BT refitting has cross-role ranking error at least
\(25\%\), with uniform weighting and predicted ties counted as
half-errors. The bound is attainable.
\end{claim}

Label buyers \(1,2\) and sellers \(3,4\).
The assignment-game oracle ranks the sole member of the scarce role
strictly above either opposite-role trader in each triple.
For example, if \(v_1\leq v_2\) and seller \(3\) has cost \(c<v_1\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
\varphi_1&=\frac{v_1-c}{6},\\
\varphi_2&=\frac{v_1-c}{6}+\frac{v_2-v_1}{2},\\
\varphi_3&=\frac{2(v_1-c)}{3}+\frac{v_2-v_1}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The seller's gaps over buyers \(1\) and \(2\) are
\((v_2-c)/2\) and \((v_1-c)/2\), respectively.
The one-buyer case is symmetric.
These credits use the classical assignment game.
\ledger{24, 25}

Let \(g^I_{ij}=r_i(I)-r_j(I)\).
The desired positive gaps are
\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
I&\text{Required positive gaps}\\ \hline
123&g^{123}_{31},\ g^{123}_{32}\\
124&g^{124}_{41},\ g^{124}_{42}\\
134&g^{134}_{13},\ g^{134}_{14}\\
234&g^{234}_{23},\ g^{234}_{24}
\end{array}
\]
For every antisymmetric comparator matrix, direct cancellation gives
two disjoint identities:
\[
g^{123}_{31}+g^{124}_{42}
+g^{134}_{13}+g^{234}_{24}=0,
\]
\[
g^{123}_{32}+g^{124}_{41}
+g^{134}_{14}+g^{234}_{23}=0.
\]
Each identity forces a negative gap unless all its gaps are zero when
none is negative. A negative gap costs one error; four zero gaps cost
two errors under half-credit tie scoring.
Each circuit therefore costs at least one error, for a total of at least
\(2/8=25\%\). The BT/win-score equivalence transfers this conclusion
to fitted BT orderings. \ledger{25}

The bound is attained. In edge order
\((12,13,14,23,24,34)\), choose
\[
a=\frac1{10}(0,1,0,-3,-1,0).
\]
All corresponding probabilities are between \(0.2\) and \(0.8\).
In the row order above, the eight desired win-score gaps are
\[
\frac1{10}(1,5,1,2,2,1,-7,-5),
\]
so exactly two are wrong. The construction does not require
exchangeability or a globally BT-consistent comparator.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 56--103.
\ledger{25}

The fixture also works with distinct reservations from Q's grid:
\[
(v_1,v_2)=(3.00,3.25),
\qquad
(c_3,c_4)=(0.75,1.00).
\]
Every desired oracle gap is then at least one currency unit.
This obstruction is not confined to near ties.
Giving the comparator only total active count cannot resolve it,
because all four states have three agents.
\ledger{25, 29}

The exact circuits, attaining comparator, and tie cases are saved in
\url{ada/small_pipeline_exact.json}.
The check in \url{ada/run_024_small_exact.log} covers all \(157\)
sign patterns with fewer than two errors.
Independent evaluation of the actual BT fits found exactly two errors
with stationarity residual approximately \(3.33\times10^{-16}\);
see \url{ada/run_033_saved_certificate_check.log}, lines 14--15.
\ledger{25, 29}

\begin{objection}
This small-team result is a failure observed in Q or P.
\end{objection}

It is neither. Q removes a buyer and seller together, so these
unbalanced triples are not generated by its balanced trade-removal
dynamics. They are an exogenous-dropout fixture using Q's valuation
domain and welfare oracle, motivated by P's changing membership.
P's actual observations might reveal the missing agent or its
capabilities, which would invalidate the fixed-input restriction.
The theorem establishes a capacity boundary under controlled
information, not measured LLM behavior. \ledger{25, 29}

\subsection{Exact calibrated recovery on every active subset}

The notes also characterize a stronger requirement than correct
ranking: reproducing every Shapley \emph{gap on its stipulated numerical
scale}. Let \(W(\emptyset)=0\) be a fixed cooperative game on \(N\).
Require a single fixed comparator, followed by population BT refitting,
to recover the restricted-game Shapley gaps for every active subset of
size at least two.

\begin{claim}
Such exact calibrated recovery is possible if and only if
\[
W(S)=\sum_{i\in S}u_i+h(|S|),
\qquad h(0)=h(1)=0.
\]
\end{claim}

Set \(u_i=W(\{i\})\).
On a two-player restriction, Shapley divides the interaction equally,
so the gap is \(u_i-u_j\).
A two-player BT fit then forces
\[
p_{ij}=\sigma(u_i-u_j).
\]
These probabilities are already exactly BT-consistent.
Every subsequent population refit therefore retains gaps \(u_i-u_j\).

To characterize games with those restricted Shapley gaps, write their
Harsanyi dividends as \(d_T\). The restricted credit is
\[
\varphi_i(I)=
\sum_{\substack{T\subseteq I\\i\in T}}\frac{d_T}{|T|}.
\]
All interaction contributions must be equal across active players.
On triples this forces the pair dividends to be equal.
Inductively, suppose dividends of orders below \(k\) depend only on
their order. On a \((k+1)\)-player set \(I\), the difference of the
interaction shares of \(i\) and \(j\) reduces to
\[
\frac{d_{I\setminus\{j\}}-d_{I\setminus\{i\}}}{k}=0.
\]
Any two \(k\)-subsets can be connected by single-element exchanges;
thus their dividends are equal. The grand-coalition dividend is
unrestricted and already a cardinality term. Consequently the
interaction game depends only on coalition size.
Conversely, an anonymous term \(h(|I|)\) gives every active player
\(h(|I|)/|I|\), and the stated comparator recovers all gaps.
\ledger{28}

This is an all-subsets, calibrated-gap characterization.
It is not an ordinal characterization, nor a characterization for only
one active count or Q's reachable families.
Exact linear-algebra checks through six players gave the predicted
dimension \(2|N|-1\).
The derivation uses classical dividend calculus, and novelty of this
particular representation statement has not been established.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 105--131.
\ledger{28, 29}

General two-additive graph games do not provide an ordinal no-loss
class. Consider
\[
W(S)=
\mathbf1_{\{1,2\}\subseteq S}
+\mathbf1_{\{3,4\}\subseteq S}.
\]
In each active triple, the intact productive pair has credit \(1/2\)
per player, and the isolated player has zero.
Its eight desired inequalities are the negatives of the dropout
fixture's inequalities, so the same circuits give a sharp \(25\%\)
error floor. A symmetric pair-synergy representation would instead
recover the credits by allocating half of each incident dividend.
That is existing interaction-based attribution, not a new Shapley
formula. It illustrates the distinction between representing cooperation
through pair contributions and representing it through pairwise wins.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 133--139.
\ledger{28}

\subsection{Certified results on Q's reachable families}

The larger comparison-and-refit class performs substantially better
on Q's reachable grid than the old static-score analysis might suggest.
Retain the earlier strict-profitable-removal criterion and uniform
state weighting. Fit an arbitrary fixed comparator separately for each
remaining count \(m\), then refit population BT within every state.
The certified cross-role error counts are
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r|r}
m&\text{States}&\text{Comparisons}&\text{Lower}&\text{Upper}\\ \hline
2&55&20&0&0\\
3&825&3270&0&0\\
4&4950&55220&1&323\\
6&19404&622936&16&9548
\end{array}
\]
Oracle ties are excluded.
The constructive upper rates at four and six per side are
\(0.585\%\) and \(1.533\%\), respectively.
The zero-error optima at two and three per side are exact for this
metric; the other intervals are loose bounds, not exact optima.
The upper constructions also make no errors on strict same-role
comparisons and have no predicted ties on the evaluated strict pairs.
\ledger{26, 27}

For four per side, the global infeasibility certificate contains
\(186\) positive rational weights; the six-per-side certificate
contains \(188\). Their signed constraint rows sum to zero exactly.
At six per side, \(16\) additional certificates use disjoint focal
pairs, yielding the stronger count lower bound.
At four per side, each individual focal-pair relaxation is feasible,
although a shared comparator cannot satisfy every pair simultaneously.
\ledger{27}

Upper counts were independently verified with integer constraint rows
and rounded integer comparator directions. Scaling these directions
into \((-1/2,1/2)\) produces valid interior probabilities without
changing ranks. They establish capacity of the pipeline, not that a
real judge learns those probabilities.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 141--158,
and \url{ada/pipeline_extension_verified.json}.
The saved rational null certificates were independently reconstructed
in \url{ada/run_033_saved_certificate_check.log}, lines 1--12.
\ledger{27, 29}

For the same fitted upper comparator at each count, the
margin-stratified results are
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r}
\text{Minimum gap}
&\text{Four per side}
&\text{Six per side}\\ \hline
\text{Any strict gap}&323/55220&9548/622936\\
0.05&80/44620&3144/472456\\
0.10&66/39664&1274/415820\\
0.25&2/26774&28/301946\\
0.50&0/12710&0/176258
\end{array}
\]
Each entry is errors divided by evaluated comparisons.
Separate comparators fitted specifically to the margin-restricted
objective attain zero cross-role errors for \emph{every} oracle gap
at least \(0.10\), at both four and six per side.
Integer feasibility checks certify those constructions.
They do not establish simultaneous correctness on omitted small gaps
or same-role orders; the same-role result belongs to the separate
all-pair constructions.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 160--170.
\ledger{27, 29}

This resolves review 21's principal objection in a way that
limits the proposed critique. The static-score lower bounds of
\(12.10\%\) at four per side and \(9.61\%\) at six per side are
\emph{not} lower bounds for P's refitting class.
Changing graph composition supplies useful context.
The remaining ranking obstruction on these reachable families is small
under the explicit weighting and concentrated near ties.
The sharp dropout theorem concerns a different family where the
representational obstruction is large. \ledger{26, 27, 29}

\subsection{Cardinal BT error is distinct from win-score error}

The order equivalence does not equate win-score gaps with BT log-score
gaps. The notes therefore fit these separately.
A cardinal construction fits fixed pair probabilities to the expected
wins implied by the oracle Shapley log-scores, then solves population
BT stationarity on every active state.
The resulting probabilities are interior without clipping.
\ledger{29}

The reported gap RMSE values are
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}
m&\text{Linear win gap}&\text{BT upper}&\text{Estimated BT lower}\\
\hline
2&0.02975&0.02975&0.02972\\
3&0.10753&0.10641&0.10269\\
4&0.11829&0.11702&0.11063\\
6&0.09769&0.09607&0.08911
\end{array}
\]
The metric averages squared errors over all unordered active pairs,
using the stipulated Shapley scale in currency units.
These cardinal constructions differ from the rank-optimized
comparators. The nonlinear stationarity residuals were below
\(1.2\times10^{-13}\); global optimality of the cardinal construction
was not established.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 174--185.
\ledger{29}

The last column is a numerical estimate of a mathematical lower bound,
not an interval-certified endpoint.
For an active set of \(n\) agents, define
\[
f_i(c)=
\sum_{j\ne i}
\left[\sigma(c_i-c_j)-\frac12\right].
\]
Its Jacobian is a complete-graph Laplacian with edge weights at most
\(1/4\), hence spectral norm at most \(n/4\).
Consequently, the BT gap RMSE is at least \(4/n\) times the best linear
fit's RMSE to the oracle moment gaps
\[
f_i(\varphi)-f_j(\varphi).
\]
The inequality is mathematical; the sigmoid targets and least-squares
computations producing the tabulated lower estimates use floating-point
arithmetic. No interval-arithmetic certificates were supplied.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 187--193,
and \url{ada/run_028_population_bt_gaps.log}.
\ledger{29}

\section{Additional sources and implementation search}

The extension checked the following antecedents, as recorded in
\url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 195--206:
\begin{itemize}
\item Stern and Tettenhorst:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.08318}.
The pooled LaTeX reading at lines 443--500 concerns Hodge decomposition
on the coalition hypercube and its relation to Shapley values.
That graph differs from P's graph of individual agents.
A general connexion between Hodge methods and Shapley values is
therefore prior work.
\item Jiang et al.:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/0811.1067}.
Pooled LaTeX lines 1546--1561 and 2030--2045 relate complete-graph
least-squares ranking to Borda scores and analyze cyclic edge components.
The present linear feasibility audit uses fixed antisymmetric edge
flows on changing subsets; it does not introduce a new Hodge
decomposition.
\item Anderson et al.,
\emph{Relationships between Borda Voting and Zermelo Ranking}:
\url{https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5830124/}.
The primary HTML was inspected for the order equivalence.
Pool retrieval by title and URL returned HTTP 429; no separate PDF
was downloaded.
\item Grabisch,
\emph{k-order Additive Discrete Fuzzy Measures and Their Representation}:
\url{https://doi.org/10.1016/S0165-0114(97)00168-1}.
The publisher abstract and author's bibliography were accessible,
but full-text pool retrieval twice returned HTTP 429.
Its full text remains unchecked.
The dividend arguments above are supplied explicitly rather than
attributed to an unread theorem.
\end{itemize}
\ledger{29}

These searches did not identify a source
establishing the exact dropout fixture or calibrated representation
characterization. This is a limited search record, not proof of novelty.
Classical glove markets, assignment games, dividend calculus,
context-sensitive choice, and coalition-aware attribution remain
essential attribution. The subsequent social-choice check below further
narrows the prospective novelty. \ledger{17, 28, 29, 36}

No matching public P repository was found through exact-title,
author, and implementation searches. The supplied source and the checked
ACL landing page,
\url{https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1349/},
provided no repository link. Similarly named repositories were unrelated.
This does not prove that P has no public code.
Actual query context, regularization, reward masks, and comparison
samples remain uninspected.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, line 206.
\ledger{29}

\section{Open questions retained from the preceding round}

The preceding account established a representational boundary between
pairwise ranking and coalition-sensitive credit. The dropout fixture
has an exact \(25\%\) population error floor for the stated fixed-input
class, while permissive comparators recover almost all strict
rankings on the separately audited reachable Q families.
The calibrated all-subsets characterization provides a separate
structural result. Neither establishes publication readiness;
novelty of the representation statements remained unconfirmed.
\ledger{25, 27, 28, 29}

The empirical material was still thin. No inference endpoint appeared,
no real judge was measured, and neither paper's trading or learning
results were replicated. The constructed optimal or feasible
comparators are not simulated LLM measurements.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, lines 208--218.
\ledger{14, 29}

Unresolved questions concerned whether P's actual observations already
supply equivalent coalition context; whether its implemented estimator
uses a prior or bounds; how frequently relevant states occur; and
whether improving semantic credit improves finite-budget learning.
Correct potential-based shaping can preserve the objective despite an
uninformative potential, so semantic rank error alone does not establish
changed optimal policies. P's conditional Shapley proposition and its
reported learning curves are not refuted.
\ledger{21, 29}

\section{The preceding next-step proposal}

The preceding evidence made the four dropout triples the first
discriminating judge test, with reachable Q-grid families as a contrast.
Freeze pair-visible trader cards, randomize aliases and presentation
order, and compare pair-only inputs, total-count inputs, and
coalition-capability context. Hold the judge and an explicitly specified
regularized BT fitter fixed. Vary repetition separately from information,
reporting both fixed-query and matched-token budgets.
See \url{ada/iterate_21_pipeline.md}, line 214.
\ledger{29}

Estimate raw pair probabilities as well as fitted rankings, and check
whether the first two arms actually preserve the fixed-comparator
condition. Their population floor is known for the dropout fixture.
Capability context removes that restriction; the ideal contextual
comparator
\[
p_{ij}(I)=
\sigma\bigl(\varphi_i(I)-\varphi_j(I)\bigr)
\]
recovers all population gaps, providing an exact attainable target.
Finite-query graph separation and potential entropy should remain
controls, rather than being conflated with semantic error.
\ledger{25, 29}

Recovery with capability context, persisting under the budget controls,
would support the proposed intervention. If existing observations
already reveal the required context, the diagnosis becomes a restriction
on artificial pair-only inputs. If semantic improvements fail to help
learning, the contribution remains an attribution benchmark rather than
a demonstrated MARL improvement.
The ideal comparator above assumes the oracle answer; review 31
correctly required a constructive repair that does not make that
assumption. The following extension supplies it for the homogeneous
family. \ledger{29, 31, 37}

\section{Appended extension after ITERATE 31}
\label{sec:family-extension}

The verifier re-derived the small-team circuits, attaining
comparator, dividend characterization, and moment bound, but again judged
the account insufficient for paper review. The material objections were
that the subset-ranking framework had not been checked against Saari's
social-choice literature, that the cardinal characterization was narrow,
and that the favorable grid results used oracle-fitted comparators
separately for each active count. The small late-round families also
contained many oracle ties. \ledger{31}

The requested extension therefore pursued family-level error bounds,
minimal context and query order, and a shared comparator across all
reachable counts. No judge request was repeated: no inference endpoint
appeared, and no new implementation evidence about P became available.
The resulting account is
\url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 5--35.
\ledger{32, 39}

\subsection{The ordinal framework is classical}

The general feasibility question is the Borda-dictionary question:
which rankings over candidate subsets can arise from a common preference
profile? Saari's dictionary work already studies this question.
Permitting arbitrary pair probabilities does not create a new ordinal
capacity class. \ledger{36}

For a rational skew margin direction, a classical McGarvey two-voter
edge construction changes one selected pair's margin while cancelling
all other margins. Combining such blocks realizes a positive common
multiple of the desired rational direction. Dividing by electorate size
uniformly scales the matrix and preserves every subset row-sum ranking.
Conversely, every preference profile yields a skew margin matrix.
Thus the present strict-order feasibility problem belongs to the
established Borda-dictionary framework. The linear inequalities,
positive circuit certificates, and Borda/BT reduction are tools used
here, not new representation machinery.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 19--25.
\ledger{36}

The remaining candidate contribution is the quantitative distance of a
specified coalition-credit target family from that class, together with
constructive repairs. Whether its exact formula already appears in the
older literature remains unresolved. \ledger{36, 39}

\subsection{The homogeneous dropout family}

Let \(N=B\cup S\) contain \(m\) identical buyers and \(m\) identical
sellers, and use the classical game
\[
W(C)=\nu\min\{b(C),s(C)\},
\qquad \nu>0.
\]
For \(1\leq d<m\), let \(\mathcal F_{m,d}\) contain every state obtained
by deleting exactly \(d\) buyers or exactly \(d\) sellers. It contains
\(2\binom md\) states, each with \(2m-d\) active agents and
\(m(m-d)\) cross-role comparisons. Weight states and their cross-role
pairs uniformly. \ledger{33, 34}

Within each state, members of the scarce role have strictly larger
restricted-game Shapley credit. Same-role members are equal.
The notes justify this through the Shapley pair-difference formula:
for a focal buyer and seller, the difference of their marginal
contributions to a predecessor set with \(x\) other buyers and \(y\)
other sellers is
\[
\nu\,\operatorname{sign}(y-x).
\]
The pair-difference weights admit a common Bernoulli-inclusion mixture.
Coupling equal-sized buyer and seller parts symmetrically, then adding
the excess role's Bernoulli variables, gives a strict advantage to
the scarce focal role. Exact rational checks cover counts one through
ten per role. See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md},
lines 55--67, and
\url{ada/run_041_dropout_context_verify.log}, line 1.
\ledger{37, 39}

\begin{claim}
For every \(m\geq2\) and \(1\leq d<m\), the exact minimum cross-role
ranking error over \(\mathcal F_{m,d}\), among arbitrary fixed reciprocal
comparators followed by complete, equally weighted population BT
refitting, is
\[
\frac{1}{2(d+1)}.
\]
Probabilities are interior, oracle comparisons are strict, and predicted
ties count as half-errors.
\end{claim}

This permits arbitrary identity-specific probabilities and
inconsistencies. It does not assume role exchangeability.
The theorem concerns frozen pair-input distributions, not P's measured
judgments. \ledger{34, 39}

\subsection{Circuit proof of the lower bound}

Choose \(U\subseteq B\) and \(V\subseteq S\), each of size \(d+1\),
and a bijection \(\pi:U\to V\). Set
\[
H=N\setminus(U\cup V).
\]
For \(i\in U\) and \(j=\pi(i)\), define
\[
I_i=H\cup\{i\}\cup V,
\qquad
J_j=H\cup U\cup\{j\}.
\]
Both are in \(\mathcal F_{m,d}\). Buyer \(i\) is in the scarce role
in \(I_i\), and seller \(j\) is in the scarce role in \(J_j\).
Using the previously defined win-score gap \(g^I_{ij}\), every term in
\[
\sum_{i\in U}
\left(
g^{I_i}_{i,\pi(i)}
+
g^{J_{\pi(i)}}_{\pi(i),i}
\right)=0
\]
should be positive. The identity nevertheless holds for every skew
matrix. Contributions from \(H\) cancel pair by pair, cross-role totals
cancel across the bijection, and within-role totals vanish by
antisymmetry. \ledger{34, 39}

Each circuit contains \(2(d+1)\) desired inequalities and costs at least
one error. A negative gap costs one; if no gap is negative, all must
be zero, costing \(d+1\) half-tie errors. The number of circuits and
the number containing any fixed desired inequality are respectively
\[
C=\binom{m}{d+1}^{2}(d+1)!,
\qquad
L=\binom{m-1}{d}d!.
\]
Double counting therefore gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{total ranking loss}&\geq C/L,\\
\frac{\text{total ranking loss}}
     {2\binom md\,m(m-d)}
&\geq\frac{1}{2(d+1)}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The \(m=2,d=1\) case recovers the two small-team circuits.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 69--106.
\ledger{34, 39}

\subsection{An explicit attaining comparator}

Index sellers by \(j=0,\ldots,m-1\). Define
\[
\begin{aligned}
b_{jk}
&=\operatorname{sign}(j-k)
  (m+1)^{\max\{j,k\}+1},
\qquad b_{jj}=0,\\
X_j&=1+\sum_k b_{jk}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Construct an integer skew direction \(A\) by setting buyer--buyer entries
to zero, every buyer's margin against seller \(j\) to \(X_j\), and
\[
A_{S_j,S_k}=(2m-d)b_{jk}-X_j+X_k.
\]
Normalize by
\[
a_{ij}=\frac{A_{ij}}{4\max_{u,v}|A_{uv}|},
\qquad p_{ij}=\frac12+a_{ij}.
\]
These probabilities lie in \([1/4,3/4]\); scaling preserves signs.
\ledger{34, 39}

Before scaling, every desired buyer--seller gap in a buyer-dropout
state equals \(2m-d>0\). In a seller-dropout state with deleted set
\(D\), the desired gap of remaining seller \(j\) over any buyer is
\[
-(2m-d)\left(1+\sum_{k\in D}b_{jk}\right).
\]
For each \((d+1)\)-seller set \(T=D\cup\{j\}\), exactly its
largest-index member has a positive row sum against the others.
Every other member has a row sum below \(-1\), because its negative
edge to the largest index dominates its smaller positive edges.
Consequently, exactly one choice of remaining seller in each such
set is wrong. The seller-dropout half has error \(1/(d+1)\);
the buyer-dropout half has zero error. The combined error is
\(1/[2(d+1)]\). \ledger{34, 39}

The construction is a capacity witness, not a proposed judge.
Its normalized margins can become small as the population grows.
Population attainability therefore does not imply good finite-query
performance. A separate polynomial-sized construction for \(d=1\)
is recorded in \url{ada/verify_dropout_context.py}.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 108--135.
\ledger{39}

Exact construction checks cover every \(2\leq m\leq10\) and
\(1\leq d<m\); all circuits and incidence counts were exhaustively
checked through \(m=5\).
The saved checks are
\url{ada/general_dropout_verified.json} and
\url{ada/run_045_general_dropout_verify.log}, lines 1--45.
Earlier MIP searches supplied witnesses rather than proofs; the analytic
construction supersedes the timed-out search.
\ledger{39}

For one departure, the floor is \(25\%\) at every population size.
For fixed \(d\), it persists as \(m\) grows. For \(d=m-1\), it is
\(1/(2m)\), so it vanishes as the dropout count grows with the
population. A blanket \(25\%\) claim for multiple departures would
therefore be false. \ledger{33, 34}

The notes also give a lower bound for symmetric mixed-departure strata.
When both orientations of unequal residual counts \(b,s\) are included,
averaging embedded balanced pools gives a conditional lower bound
\(1/[2(|b-s|+1)]\). This is not an exact optimum across arbitrary
mixtures: simultaneous attainment across count strata was not proved.
Balanced strata contain only cross-role oracle ties and are excluded
from the strict-order metric.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, line 137.
\ledger{39}

\subsection{Minimal global context for this family}

The homogeneous family admits a repair that does not assume numerical
Shapley values. Let
\[
z=\operatorname{sign}(s-b)
\]
identify the scarce role, and choose \(0<\epsilon<1/2\). Use
\[
\begin{aligned}
p_{B_i,S_j}&=\frac12+\epsilon z,\\
p_{B_i,B_k}&=p_{S_j,S_\ell}=\frac12.
\end{aligned}
\]
A buyer has centered win score \(s\epsilon z\), and a seller has
centered win score \(-b\epsilon z\). Their gap has the correct scarce-role
sign, so population BT ranks every cross-role pair correctly.
\ledger{37}

Within \(\mathcal F_{m,d}\), \(z\) has two possible values.
One global bit therefore suffices. A context alphabet with one value
reduces to the fixed-comparator class, which has positive error by the
theorem. Thus one bit is minimal within this specified global-context
protocol. Including balanced states requires a third symbol if exact
cross-role ties are also required.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 139--151.
\ledger{37, 39}

This is not an information lower bound for arbitrary aggregators.
An aggregator supplied the roster and role labels can count roles
itself, replace BT, and recover the ranking without any LMM call.
Total active count alone cannot distinguish the two regimes here:
every state has \(2m-d\) members.
Role counts suffice for the homogeneous game, not the heterogeneous
assignment game in general. \ledger{37, 39}

\subsection{A third observation can replace the global summary}

For a buyer--seller query, sample a third active agent uniformly.
Use the fixed local rule that favors the role appearing once in the
triple: give centered margin \(+\epsilon\) when the witness is a seller
and \(-\epsilon\) when it is a buyer. Same-role comparisons tie.
Averaging the witness gives
\[
a_{B_i,S_j}(I)
=\epsilon\,\frac{s-b}{b+s-2}.
\]
Population BT consequently has the correct scarce-role ordering
for every coalition with at least three active agents. A two-agent
buyer--seller coalition returns a tie.
This rule uses roles and a witness, not an oracle credit.
\ledger{37}

An equivalent deterministic construction sums local scores over active
triples. Give \(2\) to the scarce-role member and \(-1\) to each of the
other two; give zero on all-same-role triples.
The total scores are
\[
\begin{aligned}
t_B&=2\binom{s}{2}-(b-1)s=s(s-b),\\
t_S&=2\binom{b}{2}-(s-1)b=b(b-s),\\
t_B-t_S&=(b+s)(s-b).
\end{aligned}
\]
These scores recover the Shapley ordering, not its numerical values.
Thus two-agent information is insufficient for zero error on the
adverse family within the frozen-input BT protocol, while
three-agent information suffices.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 153--171.
\ledger{37, 39}

\subsection{Costs and the direct-attribution baseline}

For \(n\) active agents, P-style ordered pair comparisons use
\(n(n-1)\) calls per repetition. Adding a scarcity bit preserves
this call count but requires a role census.
Adding one sampled witness also preserves call count, while increasing
each call from two observations to three. Exhaustive triple outputs
require \(\binom n3\) calls, or \(6\binom n3\) if all presentation
orders are queried. In the latter comparison, the call-count ratio to
ordered pairs is \(n-2\). \ledger{39}

A sampled witness gives the correct population target but adds
sampling noise. Near balance, its expected margin is of order \(1/n\).
At fixed local confidence, an elementary uniform Hoeffding bound for
independent pair estimates gives a conservative sufficient repetition
count of order
\[
n^2\log(n/\delta)
\]
for failure probability \(\delta\).
This is not an optimal sample-complexity theorem or an observation
about real multimodal accuracy. The inputs and token budgets must be
counted alongside calls.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 173--183.
\ledger{39}

For a known symbolic glove game, directly reading and counting the
\(n\) role labels is cheaper and gives exact ordinal credit without pair
calls. Exact cardinal credits follow from the glove formula.
The bit and witness constructions establish representational sufficiency;
they are not competitive algorithms for an already known symbolic
market. \ledger{37, 39}

For Q's heterogeneous opportunity game, role counts alone are
insufficient: the earlier balanced examples have identical counts and
focal features but opposite credit gaps.
A histogram of buyer values and seller costs is sufficient.
The threshold decomposition already derived gives a computable,
non-oracular route: obtain eligible counts at each threshold, look up
the corresponding glove credit, and integrate over thresholds for
which each trader qualifies. On Q's grid, ten threshold count pairs
suffice. With a precomputed glove table, a sorted sweep and prefix sums
evaluate all credits.
Histogram sufficiency is established; its minimality is not.
Giving these private values to Q's traders would change Q's experiment,
so the proposed use is offline evaluation or diagnostic judge context.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 185--203.
\ledger{39}

For an arbitrary game accessible only through coalition-value queries,
exhaustive evaluation is a generic \(2^n\)-query baseline, with
permutation sampling as an approximate alternative.
For a known \(k\)-additive game, values on coalitions of size at most
\(k\), followed by classical M\"obius inversion and dividend sharing,
recover the credits. These are existing attribution constructions.
\ledger{39}

Required observation order does not simply equal Harsanyi interaction
order. The two-additive disjoint-pairs game fails the antisymmetric
pair-win protocol, although symmetric pair-value queries recover its
credits. Conversely, the glove game has higher-order dividends,
yet the triple rule recovers its ordinal ranking.
Query semantics, the known game class, and aggregation matter as well
as interaction order.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 205--209.
\ledger{39}

\subsection{One comparator shared across every reachable Q count}

The scope gap in the earlier grid fits is now closed constructively.
One skew matrix on the same 22 trader identities was fitted jointly
across every active count from one through eleven per side, then
used unchanged in every per-state population BT refit.
The strictly profitable-removal family contains \(58{,}786\) states.
The fitting objective pools strict comparisons; these are structural
enumeration weights, not observed Q visitation frequencies.
See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 211--243.
\ledger{35, 39}

The integer-verified construction gives:
\[
\begin{array}{r|r|r|r|r}
m&\text{States}&\text{Strict cross-role}&
\text{Oracle ties}&\text{Errors}\\ \hline
1&1&0&1&0\\
2&55&20&200&0\\
3&825&3270&4155&68\\
4&4950&55220&23980&956\\
5&13860&285500&61000&4726\\
6&19404&622936&75608&9522\\
7&13860&632982&46158&8610\\
8&4950&302190&14610&3148\\
9&825&64452&2373&388\\
10&55&5304&196&18\\
11&1&110&11&0
\end{array}
\]
The pooled strict cross-role error is
\[
\frac{27436}{1971984}\approx1.3913\%.
\]
There are no predicted ties on these strict cross-role comparisons.
This is a feasible upper bound, not an optimum or measured judge
performance. The optimizer stopped at its 350-iteration cap; integer
verification makes the construction valid independently of convergence.
The saved matrix is in \url{ada/shared_count_fit.json}.
\ledger{35, 39}

The shared fit has two same-role errors, both at three agents per side.
Entry 35's initial assertion of no same-role errors was incorrect and
was explicitly corrected in entry 38. The saved results already contained
the correct count. Independent matrix-row reconstruction confirmed the
correction; see \url{ada/run_050_shared_independent_check.log},
lines 18--24. The earlier separately fitted comparators and their
zero same-role error statements remain distinct results.
\ledger{27, 38, 39}

The small-family caveat is substantial. At two per side, only \(20\) of
\(220\) cross-role comparisons are strict; at three per side, only
\(3{,}270\) of \(7{,}425\) are strict. Many traders are dummies in these
low-surplus residual markets. The shared fit also no longer achieves
zero error at three per side.
These facts prevent interpreting the earlier exact zero-error results
as evidence of broad judge competence. \ledger{31, 35, 39}

For the newly emphasized remaining counts, the same shared comparator
has the following wrong-comparison counts after filtering by oracle
gap:
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r}
m&|\Delta|\geq0.10&|\Delta|\geq0.25\\ \hline
5&952&58\\
6&1332&36\\
7&594&0\\
8&106&0\\
9&8&0
\end{array}
\]
These are counts, not percentages; full denominators and other thresholds
are retained in \url{ada/shared_count_fit.json}.
Independent reconstruction checked every state and every saved margin
table. See \url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 231--243,
and \url{ada/run_050_shared_independent_check.log}.
\ledger{39}

The older rational infeasibility certificates remain lower bounds on
their corresponding subfamilies. They are still loose.
The shared upper construction establishes that the permissive
fixed-comparator class can express most strict oracle rankings on this
grid. It was fitted directly to those oracle rankings and has no
demonstrated generalization to new identities or schedules.
It neither predicts LLM performance nor weakens the exact theorem for
the different, unbalanced dropout family. \ledger{35, 39}

\section{Sources added in the family-extension round}

The shared pool helper was used for the new paper requests.
Saari's 2022 source and CASI were available as pooled LaTeX and read
in bounded slices; no PDF-to-text extraction was used for them.
The retrieval record and limitations are in
\url{ada/iterate_31_context_order.md}, lines 245--257.
\ledger{36, 39}

\begin{itemize}
\item Saari, \emph{A Dictionary for Voting Paradoxes} (1989):
\url{https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(89)90037-9}.
The indexed publisher abstract describes coordinated outcomes over
subsets. Full-text retrieval returned HTTP 429.

\item Saari, \emph{The Borda Dictionary} (1990):
\url{https://www.jstor.org/stable/41105961}.
The indexed discussion-paper copy is
\url{https://www.kellogg.northwestern.edu/research/math/papers/821.pdf}.
Bibliographic information and abstract-level scope were checked.
The helper returned HTTP 429, and the old author URL returned 404.
Full text remains missing.

\item Saari, \emph{Mathematical Structure of Voting Paradoxes I} (2000):
\url{https://doi.org/10.1007/s001990050001}.
The author's indexed abstract,
\url{https://ssrn.com/abstract=195743},
covers candidate additions and deletions.
Full-text retrieval returned HTTP 429.

\item McGarvey (1953):
\url{https://doi.org/10.2307/1907926}.
Primary journal metadata was located, but pool resolution found no open
PDF. The edge-gadget argument is described above; the original proof
was not inspected.

\item Debord (1987):
\url{https://www.numdam.org/item/MSH_1987__97__5_0/}.
The primary bibliographic page was checked; full-text retrieval returned
HTTP 429. Its theorem wording was not verified.

\item Saari (2022):
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.13230}.
Pooled LaTeX lines 217--253 establish the skew-margin row-sum and
Borda correspondence; lines 871--885 identify older references.
This supports the attribution, not novelty of the exact error formula.

\item CASI:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20293}.
Pooled LaTeX lines 552--575 and 928--973 describe fixed-snapshot
coalition evaluation and distinguish set optimization from
coalition-dependent attribution. They also establish that singleton,
leave-one-out, and full-set values do not determine arbitrary Shapley
allocations. Its query model differs from the fixed-comparator audit,
but direct coalition attribution is an existing baseline.
\end{itemize}
\ledger{36, 39}

The search found the relevant classical framework and related attribution
work. It did not yield a checked source containing the exact
\(1/[2(d+1)]\) formula. This is a retrieval limitation and search record,
not a novelty clearance. Even if the formula is new, its proof is
elementary; publication significance would require a carefully positioned
theory or benchmark contribution.
\ledger{36, 39}

\section{Open questions retained after the family extension}

The strongest established result at that stage was a family-level capacity
theorem: the specified pair-only comparison-and-refit protocol has exact
cross-role error \(1/[2(d+1)]\) on homogeneous one-role dropout families.
One scarcity bit or a sampled third-agent rule removes population
ordinal error in that family. A capability histogram gives exact
computable credits for the heterogeneous assignment oracle.
These are conditional mathematical and computational results.
\ledger{34, 37, 39}

The countervailing result is equally important: a single arbitrary
comparator shared across every Q-reachable count makes only
\(1.3913\%\) strict cross-role errors under the pooled enumeration metric.
There is no broad impossibility result saying that pair-local refitting
cannot approximate Q's contribution rankings. The strongest adverse
family uses independently changing participation, while Q's actual
trade removals remain balanced. \ledger{35, 39}

The material is empirically thin. No real judge, MARL run, or Q trajectory
was measured. No inference endpoint appeared, and P's actual query
context, regularization, and reward implementation remain uninspected.
Population witnesses may require many samples when their margins are
small. The sampled-witness repair has no measured finite-query behavior
with LMMs, and none of the semantic improvements has a demonstrated
learning benefit. \ledger{14, 29, 39}

The exact formula still needs comparison with the unavailable
social-choice full texts. There was no general structural ordinal
classification of arbitrary cooperative games and active-set families
beyond the classical linear feasibility criterion and the special
families analyzed here. Minimal capability context for heterogeneous
markets, optimal mixed-dropout error, and empirical state weighting
remained unresolved. The calibrated characterization retained its strong
all-subsets cardinal requirement.
\ledger{28, 31, 36, 39}

Neither P's conditional Shapley proposition nor either paper's reported
trading or learning results is refuted. A poor potential can preserve
the optimal objective under valid potential-based shaping.
The mask identity and Ford-condition probabilities remain useful
secondary controls, with their stated assumptions.
\ledger{19, 29, 39}

\section{The next step proposed after the family extension}

For novelty, the smallest remaining documentary step was to obtain and
compare the older Borda-dictionary and margin-realizability texts against
the explicit dropout circuits and error formula. That would settle
whether the prospective theorem is already known; the retrieval
record did not. \ledger{36, 39}

For empirical relevance, once inference access exists, use the four
dropout triples as the smallest discriminating experiment.
Freeze focal cards and task text, randomize aliases and presentation
order, and compare pair-only inputs, total-count inputs, a scarce-role
bit, and a sampled third-agent observation. Use the same judge and
explicitly regularized BT estimator. Vary repetitions independently
of information, and report both fixed-call and matched-token budgets.
Include direct symbolic role counting as the baseline.
\ledger{29, 37, 39}

Estimate raw pair probabilities before interpreting aggregate rankings.
If the pair-only distribution changes with unseen teammates, the
fixed-comparator premise does not describe that implementation.
If it remains fixed, the population floor provides a calibrated target;
the context and witness arms test whether actual judges can realize the
constructive repairs. Separation frequency and potential entropy should
remain finite-query controls. \ledger{29, 39}

This test would settle the immediate question of whether supplying
coalition information produces the predicted semantic separation in real
judges. It would not establish embodied transfer or improved learning.
Those would require a subsequent implementation and training study.
The supported account at that stage was an exact conditional boundary and
an oracle-grounded benchmark proposal, with a favorable reachable-grid
capacity result preserved alongside it. \ledger{39}

\section{Appended extension after ITERATE 41}
\label{sec:general-extension}

The latest verifier accepted the dropout-family formula, its attaining
construction, the context repairs, and the contrasting grid fit under
their stated conditions. It nevertheless judged the account not ready
for paper review, scoring it \([rating withheld]\). Its material concerns were
unconfirmed novelty inside a classical framework, reliance on a special
glove family, lack of a general game account, and absence of measured
judges. It proposed a bounded exploratory round using balanced
contributions, membership dependence, local query order, and independent
participation. \ledger{41, 42}

The resulting work is recorded in
\url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 1--397.
It strengthens the general mathematical account but does not settle
novelty or empirical relevance. No inference endpoint appeared, no judge
measurement was invented, and no grid fitting was repeated.
The preceding claims and qualifications remain in force.
\ledger{43, 46, 47, 49, 50}

\subsection{Balanced contributions and antisymmetric wins}

Let \(N\) contain \(n\geq2\) identities and let \(W(\emptyset)=0\).
Write the game's Harsanyi dividends as \(d_T\):
\[
W(I)=\sum_{T\subseteq I}d_T,
\qquad
\varphi_i(I)=
\sum_{\substack{T\subseteq I\\i\in T}}\frac{d_T}{|T|}.
\]
The classical cooperative potential is
\[
\Psi(I)=
\sum_{\emptyset\ne T\subseteq I}\frac{d_T}{|T|},
\qquad
\varphi_i(I)=\Psi(I)-\Psi(I\setminus\{i\}).
\]
Its mixed differences commute, giving balanced contributions:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\varphi_i(I)-\varphi_i(I\setminus\{j\})
&=\sum_{\substack{T\subseteq I\\i,j\in T}}\frac{d_T}{|T|}\\
&=\varphi_j(I)-\varphi_j(I\setminus\{i\}).
\end{aligned}
\]
These are established cooperative-game identities associated with
Myerson and Hart and Mas-Colell. The set potential \(\Psi\) is not
P's temporal reward-shaping potential. \ledger{49, 50}

A fixed reciprocal comparator instead gives
\[
r_i(I)=\sum_{j\in I\setminus\{i\}}a_{ij},
\qquad
r_i(I)-r_i(I\setminus\{j\})=a_{ij}=-a_{ji}.
\]
Shared cooperative contributions and pairwise wins therefore have
different membership increments. This observation alone proves no
general gap or ranking impossibility: a score vector can be translated
separately in each coalition, and positive rescaling preserves rankings.
The following results explicitly distinguish those freedoms.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 39--80.
\ledger{43, 49, 50}

\subsection{Decomposing arbitrary-game win-gap loss}

Fix a focal pair \(i,j\), and let \(x_k\) indicate participation of each
other agent. The restricted-game Shapley gap is the multilinear polynomial
\[
h_{ij}(x)=
\sum_{S\subseteq N\setminus\{i,j\}}
\frac{d_{S\cup\{i\}}-d_{S\cup\{j\}}}{|S|+1}
\prod_{k\in S}x_k.
\]
Dividends containing both focal agents cancel. A fixed-comparator
win gap has the more restrictive form
\[
g_{ij}(x)=2a_{ij}
+\sum_{k\ne i,j}(a_{ik}-a_{jk})x_k.
\]
Thus polynomial degree is not the only restriction: the linear
coefficients must arise from one common skew matrix. \ledger{43, 50}

Suppose agents participate independently with common probability
\(p\in(0,1)\). Conditional on the focal pair being present, define
\[
\mathcal L_p(a)=
\sum_{i<j}\mathbb E\!\left[
  \bigl(h_{ij}(X)-g_{ij}(X)\bigr)^2
  \,\middle|\, i,j\text{ present}\right].
\]
The unconditional expected sum over active pairs is
\(p^2\mathcal L_p(a)\). Dividing by \(\binom n2\) gives the pooled
pair mean squared error. This differs from weighting every state equally
regardless of its number of pairs. \ledger{43, 50}

Put \(v=p(1-p)\). Expanding around \(x_k=p\), define, for
\(U\subseteq N\setminus\{i,j\}\),
\[
H_{ij,U}=
\sum_{\substack{S\subseteq N\setminus\{i,j\}\\S\supseteq U}}
\frac{d_{S\cup\{i\}}-d_{S\cup\{j\}}}{|S|+1}
p^{|S|-|U|}.
\]
The products of centered independent indicators are orthogonal.
Writing \(R_i=\sum_{j\ne i}a_{ij}\), the loss is exactly
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L_p(a)
={}&\sum_{i<j}
 \bigl[H_{ij,\emptyset}
       -2(1-p)a_{ij}-p(R_i-R_j)\bigr]^2\\
&+v\sum_{i<j}\sum_{k\ne i,j}
 \bigl[H_{ij,\{k\}}-a_{ik}+a_{jk}\bigr]^2\\
&+\sum_{i<j}
  \sum_{\substack{U\subseteq N\setminus\{i,j\}\\|U|\geq2}}
  v^{|U|}H_{ij,U}^{\,2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The last line is irreducible higher-order membership energy.
The first two lines retain compatibility restrictions even when the
target is affine. A two-additive game has no last-line contribution,
but its shared pair dividends can still be incompatible with skew
win interactions. See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md},
lines 82--128. \ledger{43, 50}

\subsection{Closed-form projection and residual}

The optimization here allows unrestricted real skew directions.
It is a relaxation of fixed-scale probability margins constrained to
\([-1/2,1/2]\), and therefore gives a lower bound for that constrained
cardinal model. It is exact for linear win scores with a freely chosen
global calibration scale: a finite direction can be scaled into
interior probabilities and its scores rescaled. No such calibration
is silently inserted into P's BT log-scores. \ledger{43, 50}

Define
\[
\begin{aligned}
\alpha&=2+(n-2)p,\\
\lambda&=\alpha^2+(n-2)p(1-p),\\
z_i&=\sum_{T\ni i}\frac{d_T}{|T|}p^{|T|-1}
       \bigl[\alpha+(|T|-1)(1-p)\bigr].
\end{aligned}
\]

\begin{claim}
For every game \(W\) and common participation rate \(0<p<1\),
the unique unrestricted skew minimizer is
\[
a^\star_{ij}=\frac{z_i-z_j}{\lambda}.
\]
In particular, its triangle circulations vanish.
\end{claim}

Thus cyclic comparator components cannot improve this particular
cardinal objective. This does not contradict their usefulness for
ordinal fitting on selected active families, demonstrated by the
earlier grid audit. \ledger{43, 50}

For the derivation, use the edge norm
\[
\|a\|_E^2=\sum_{i<j}a_{ij}^2
\]
and decompose skew matrices into gradients \(a_{ij}=u_i-u_j\),
with \(\sum_i u_i=0\), and divergence-free cycles \(R_i=0\).
The constant-coefficient map is
\[
(L_0a)_{ij}=2(1-p)a_{ij}+p(R_i-R_j).
\]
It multiplies gradients by \(\alpha\) and cycles by \(2(1-p)\).
For the linear-coefficient map,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\|L_1a\|^2
&=\sum_{i<j}\sum_{k\ne i,j}(a_{ik}-a_{jk})^2\\
&=2(n-1)\|a\|_E^2-\sum_iR_i^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
The normal operator consequently has eigenvalue \(\lambda\) on gradients
and
\[
\lambda_C=4(1-p)^2+2(n-1)p(1-p)>0
\]
on cycles.

For a single dividend \(d_T\), with \(t=|T|\), the constant target is
the gradient
\[
\frac{d_T}{t}p^{t-1}
\bigl(\mathbf1_{i\in T}-\mathbf1_{j\in T}\bigr).
\]
The linear-target adjoint is also a gradient, with coefficient
\((t-1)d_Tp^{t-2}/t\) for \(t\geq2\), and zero for \(t=1\).
Combining the constant and linear terms makes the normal-equation
right-hand side \(z_i-z_j\), with no cycle component.
Inverting the two eigenspaces proves the claim.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 130--188.
\ledger{43, 50}

The coefficient also has a participation interpretation. Let
\[
\mu_i(p)=\mathbb E[\varphi_i(I)\mid i\in I].
\]
Then
\[
z_i=\alpha\mu_i(p)+p(1-p)\mu_i'(p).
\]
The projection contrasts mean restricted credit and its sensitivity to
participation. This is a dividend-weighted score, not a claimed new
axiomatic cooperative value. \ledger{50}

For a fully explicit residual, let \(T,U\ne\emptyset\), and put
\(t=|T|\), \(u=|U|\), \(c=|T\cap U|\). Define
\[
\begin{aligned}
K_p(T,U)={}&
 \frac{c(n-t-u+c)}{tu}\,p^{t+u-c-1}\\
&-\frac{(t-c)(u-c)}{tu}\,p^{t+u-c-2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Terms with zero integer coefficient are interpreted as zero, including
any formally negative power in such a term. With
\[
E_p=\sum_{T,U\ne\emptyset}d_Td_UK_p(T,U),
\qquad
\bar z=\frac1n\sum_i z_i,
\]
the minimum is
\[
\min_a\mathcal L_p(a)
=E_p-\frac{n}{\lambda}\sum_i(z_i-\bar z)^2.
\]
To obtain \(K_p\), count pair products of two dividend gap terms.
A focal pair with one member in \(T\cap U\) and the other outside
\(T\cup U\) contributes positively; there are
\(c(n-t-u+c)\) such pairs. A pair with one member in each set difference
contributes negatively; there are \((t-c)(u-c)\).
The respective remaining-membership counts give the displayed powers.
The gradient norm identity supplies the projected energy.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 190--218.
\ledger{43, 50}

In a homogeneous glove game with equally many original buyers and
sellers, game automorphisms make every \(z_i\) equal. The optimal
linear predictor is therefore zero. This is a warning about metrics,
not a claim that ranking is impossible: the earlier constructions
achieve better-than-chance ordinal accuracy by concentrating errors.
\ledger{43, 50}

\subsection{The linear zero-loss class and the corrected sign}

\begin{claim}
On unrestricted linear win-score units, exact recovery of every
restricted Shapley gap on every active subset is possible precisely for
games
\[
W(I)=|I|\sum_{i\in I}u_i+h(|I|),
\qquad h(0)=h(1)=0,
\]
where \(u_i=W(\{i\})\).
\end{claim}

Two-agent restrictions force
\[
2a_{ij}=u_i-u_j.
\]
The coefficient of a third agent's membership then forces
\[
d_{\{i,k\}}-d_{\{j,k\}}=u_i-u_j,
\]
so the pair dividends have the form
\[
d_{\{i,j\}}=u_i+u_j+c.
\]
The sign is positive under this singleton-value convention.
The tentative negative-sum expression in review 41 is not correct with
these parameters. Exact checks of the positive construction give zero
residual, whereas the tested negative-sum counterparts give positive
residual. \ledger{44}

For each dividend order from three through \(n-1\), vanishing
higher-order membership coefficients forces equal dividends on subsets
differing by one element, hence equality across that order.
The grand-coalition dividend is unrestricted and already anonymous.
Summing these conditions yields the displayed game class.
Conversely, its Shapley gaps are
\[
\varphi_i(I)-\varphi_j(I)
=\frac{|I|}{2}(u_i-u_j),
\]
which \(a_{ij}=(u_i-u_j)/2\) represents exactly.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 220--245.
\ledger{44, 50}

This differs from the earlier exact BT-log-score class
\[
W(I)=\sum_{i\in I}u_i+h(|I|).
\]
Both are calibrated all-subsets statements. Neither characterizes
strict ordinal representation, and neither characterizes only the
Q-reachable family. Fixed probability bounds would impose further
scale restrictions on the linear statement. \ledger{44, 50}

\subsection{A specified local-query protocol}

To discuss query order, the notes define an additive local-output
protocol rather than all algorithms that happen to ingest \(k\) images.
For every identity subset \(U\) with \(|U|\leq k\), a frozen local
evaluator supplies scores \(q_i^U\) satisfying
\[
\sum_{i\in U}q_i^U=0.
\]
The state score is
\[
R_i(I)=
\sum_{\substack{U\subseteq I,\ i\in U\\|U|\leq k}}q_i^U.
\]
Binary reciprocal comparisons are the \(k=2\) case.
Every fixed-pair gap has membership degree at most \(k-1\), but
its coefficients must also come from common locally zero-sum blocks.
\ledger{46, 50}

Each block has a within-context skew-margin realization:
\[
a_{ij}^U=\frac{q_i^U-q_j^U}{|U|},
\qquad
\sum_{j\in U\setminus\{i\}}a_{ij}^U=q_i^U.
\]
Summing these margins over active local subsets and scaling them into
interior probabilities yields a complete population BT fit with the
same ordering as \(R\). This extends P's protocol; it is not a
description of P's implemented pair-only rule. \ledger{46}

A uniformly sampled context of exactly \(k\) agents requires a
normalization qualification. Multiplication by the common positive
factor
\[
\binom{|I|-2}{k-2}
\]
turns each effective pair-margin average into its additive local sum,
when such contexts exist. The raw average may be rational in active
count, while its sign has a degree-at-most-\(k-1\) representation.
Mixing context sizes without the corresponding weights need not
preserve this form.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 247--279.
\ledger{50}

The glove ordering shows why threshold degree alone is insufficient.
Its desired cross-role direction is the sign of the linear function
\(s-b\). Binary win gaps are also linear, but their shared skew
coefficients cannot realize all these signs on the dropout family.
The ternary rule instead produces a gap proportional to
\((b+s)(s-b)\), with the same sign.
It resolves a coefficient-compatibility restriction even though the
target itself has threshold degree one. \ledger{46, 50}

\subsection{A constructive repair for every bounded-order game}

\begin{claim}
If \(d_T=0\) whenever \(|T|>d\), frozen zero-sum local outputs on
at most \(\min(d+1,n)\) agents can recover every restricted-game
Shapley ordering and tie.
\end{claim}

For each nonzero dividend \(d_T\) and every outside witness
\(k\notin T\), create a block on \(U=T\cup\{k\}\):
\[
q_i^{T,k}=\frac{d_T}{|T|}
\quad(i\in T),
\qquad
q_k^{T,k}=-d_T.
\]
Combine blocks with the same \(U\).
They are locally zero-sum. On active set \(I\), a player in
\(T\subseteq I\) receives
\((|I|-|T|)d_T/|T|\) from this dividend, while a player outside
\(T\) receives \(-d_T\). Therefore
\[
R_i(I)=|I|\varphi_i(I)-W(I).
\]
Every score gap is \(|I|\) times its Shapley gap, preserving orders
and ties. A dividend on the entire active population contributes equally
to all players and needs no outside witness for ordinal recovery.
\ledger{45, 46, 50}

For a two-additive game, singleton dividends give pair blocks
\(u_i-u_j\) and \(u_j-u_i\). The triple block is
\[
q_i^{\{i,j,k\}}
=\frac{d_{\{i,j\}}+d_{\{i,k\}}}{2}-d_{\{j,k\}}.
\]
Thus pair and triple outputs suffice for every two-additive game.
The earlier disjoint-productive-pairs fixture proves that binary
wins alone can fail even under strict-only ranking loss.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 281--311.
\ledger{28, 46, 50}

This construction starts from dividends rather than assuming an oracle
Shapley comparator. Knowing or estimating those dividends is nevertheless
a substantive input requirement. Nothing here proves that an LMM can
infer them from a small collection of egocentric images. \ledger{46, 50}

The order bound is worst-case necessary when oracle ties must be
preserved. Consider a unanimity game on \(d\) productive agents in a pool
of \(d+1\). Every proper active subset has all members tied:
the productive set alone shares its value equally, and every other
proper restriction has zero value. The full pool ranks the productive
agents above the outsider. A locally zero-sum protocol preserving all
proper-set ties must have \(R(I)=0\) on every proper subset.
M\"obius inversion forces every proper local block to vanish.
A full-set block is therefore necessary. \ledger{46}

If oracle ties are ignored, this argument gives no lower bound:
fixed scores favoring productive identities recover its only strict
rankings. The separate strict-only binary/ternary counterexample remains
necessary for that metric. \ledger{46, 50}

For exact cardinal recovery of centered Shapley allocations, the local
coefficients are uniquely determined:
\[
\begin{aligned}
q_i^U
={}&\sum_{J\subseteq U\setminus\{i\}}
 (-1)^{|U|-1-|J|}\\
&\quad\cdot\left[
 \varphi_i(J\cup\{i\})
 -\frac{W(J\cup\{i\})}{|J|+1}
 \right].
\end{aligned}
\]
Their maximum nonzero order characterizes this specific additive
representation. It need not equal dividend order. Even a heterogeneous
additive game can require high-order blocks after centering, although
binary BT comparisons recover its ordering and log-score gaps.
Positive state-dependent rescaling can greatly reduce the local order
needed for ordinal recovery.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 313--337.
\ledger{50}

\subsection{Costs and the stronger direct baseline}

For a known \(d\)-additive game supplied through coalition-value queries,
all dividends can be obtained from
\[
\sum_{t=1}^{d}\binom nt
\]
values and M\"obius inversion. Direct dividend sharing then computes
restricted Shapley credits without pair aggregation.
A literal local-output implementation may evaluate up to
\[
\sum_{t=2}^{\min(d+1,n)}\binom nt
\]
local outputs, or form
\[
\sum_{t=1}^{d}(n-t)\binom nt
\]
dividend-witness blocks before combining them.
It is not cheaper than direct attribution in this exact symbolic model.
The result establishes representation sufficiency, not computational
advantage. \ledger{46, 50}

In embodied tasks, visual comparisons and counterfactual coalition
values can differ in cost and observability. An empirical comparison
must measure those differences. The specialized scarcity-bit and
sampled-witness glove repairs remain available, and their required
order does not track the glove game's higher-order dividends.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 339--348.
\ledger{50}

\subsection{Independent participation and a logarithmic lower bound}

Consider the homogeneous glove game with \(m\) buyers and \(m\)
sellers, each independently active with common probability
\(p\in(0,1)\). Pool active cross-role comparisons across states,
exclude oracle ties, and charge predicted ties half an error.
This is an analytic participation model, not a documented law for
P's battery process. It is also different from Q's paired trade exits.
\ledger{47, 50}

The conditional circuit bound must combine the count strata
\((b,s)\) and \((s,b)\). A claim for one orientation alone would be
false: a comparator always favoring buyers succeeds whenever buyers
are always the scarce role. Equal-rate independent participation
weights the swapped strata equally. Averaging balanced subpools and
their pure-dropout circuits gives
\[
\text{conditional error}\geq
\frac{1}{2(|b-s|+1)}
\]
for their combined unequal-count stratum. \ledger{47}

Let \(B,S\) be independent \(\operatorname{Bin}(m,p)\) counts.
Pair weighting yields the lower bound
\[
L_{m,p}=
\frac{
\mathbb E\!\left[
 \dfrac{BS\,\mathbf1_{B\ne S}}{2(|B-S|+1)}
\right]}
{\mathbb E[BS\,\mathbf1_{B\ne S}]}.
\]
Size-biasing by the active focal pair simplifies it. For independent
\(X,Y\sim\operatorname{Bin}(m-1,p)\),
\[
L_{m,p}=
\frac{\displaystyle
 \sum_{d=1}^{m-1}\frac{\Pr(X-Y=d)}{d+1}}
{\Pr(X\ne Y)}.
\]
The bound applies to every fixed comparator with per-state population
BT refitting under the stated design. \ledger{47, 50}

At \(p=1/2\), the recorded exact calculations give
\[
\begin{array}{c|c|r}
m&L_{m,1/2}&\text{Decimal}\\ \hline
2&1/4&0.250000\\
3&7/30&0.233333\\
4&39/176&0.221591\\
5&593/2790&0.212545\\
10&11057031/59786720&0.184941\\
20&671161684400251/4234937130785920&0.158482
\end{array}
\]
For \(m=2\), the earlier comparator attains \(1/4\) for every
interior \(p\), since all strict states are the four equally weighted
triples. For larger populations, a single comparator attaining the
stratum bounds simultaneously has not been established.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 350--377, and
\url{ada/run_056_bernoulli_summary.log}, line 1.
\ledger{47, 50}

For fixed interior \(p\), the asymptotic lower bound is
\[
L_{m,p}\sim
\frac{\log m}{4\sqrt{\pi m p(1-p)}}.
\]
Indeed, \(X-Y\) has variance \(2(m-1)p(1-p)\).
For near-balanced differences, its local central-limit mass is
approximately
\[
\frac{1}{2\sqrt{\pi m p(1-p)}}.
\]
Summing \(1/(d+1)\) up to differences of order \(\sqrt m\)
contributes \((\log m)/2\). Differences above a fixed multiple
of \(\sqrt m\) contribute only \(O(m^{-1/2})\), and
\(\Pr(X\ne Y)\) tends to one.
At \(p=1/2\), the same argument follows from
\[
\Pr(X-Y=d)
=4^{-(m-1)}\binom{2m-2}{m-1+d}
\]
and Stirling's formula. \ledger{47, 50}

This is the decay rate of a lower bound, not the proved decay rate
of the optimum. It does not establish simultaneous attainment or
show that the obstruction disappears in large populations.
It must not be transferred to heterogeneous assignment games, where
scarcity alone does not determine every focal ordering.
The Q-grid capacity construction remains the contrasting evidence
for that family. \ledger{47, 50}

\subsection{Verification of the latest results}

The projection was checked on \(15\) rational random-game instances
through six players, at participation rates
\(1/4,1/2,3/4\).
Direct exact normal equations vanish; exact residuals agree with the
dividend kernel and an independently implemented centered-indicator
expansion. An unrestricted numerical least-squares solve agrees to
floating-point precision.
Positive-pair-sum zero-loss constructions, negative-sum counterparts,
and symmetric glove projections were checked separately.
See \url{ada/run_054_win_gap_projection.log}, lines 1--16,
and \url{ada/win_gap_projection_verified.json}.
\ledger{43, 44, 50}

The dividend-witness construction was checked on \(27\) random
bounded-order games through seven players, covering \(1{,}509\)
nonempty active subsets. The checks include within-context skew
realization and unanimity examples of orders one through six.
Entry 46's initial total of \(1{,}477\) was corrected in entry 48;
\url{ada/run_056_bernoulli_summary.log}, line 2, records the
correct total. The details are in
\url{ada/local_order_bernoulli_verified.json} and
\url{ada/run_055_local_order_bernoulli.log}.
\ledger{46, 48, 50}

Exact size-biased Bernoulli sums agree with independently weighted
original-count sums. Numerical convolution checks extend to
\(m=100000\); those checks support the calculation but are not its
asymptotic proof. These are mathematical and computational validations,
not LMM, trading, or MARL experiments.
See \url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 379--389.
\ledger{47, 50}

\section{Sources added in the latest round}

The latest attribution work precedes interpretation of the new formulas.
The shared-pool retrieval limitations are explicit:
\begin{itemize}
\item Myerson (1980), on balanced contributions:
\url{https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01781371}.
Original full-text resolution returned HTTP 429.
A later primary research article explicitly states the standard
efficiency-plus-balanced-contributions characterization:
\url{https://doi.org/10.1007/s00182-021-00791-z}.
Its publisher text was inspected. Myerson's original theorem wording
was not checked.

\item Hart and Mas-Colell (1989), on the cooperative potential:
\url{https://doi.org/10.2307/1911054}.
The author's institutional abstract was checked at
\url{https://cris.huji.ac.il/en/publications/potential-value-and-consistency/}.
The pool helper could not retrieve the author PDF,
\url{https://math1.math.huji.ac.il/~hart/papers/pot.pdf},
because resolution returned HTTP 429.
The dividend formula used here is independently derived.

\item Young (1974), on Borda axioms:
\url{https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-0531(74)90073-8}.
Original full text was not obtained.
Pooled LaTeX of Bubboloni and Gori,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.06626},
discusses net outdegree at lines 145--225 and reproduces Young's
characterization at lines 2054--2056.

\item Stern and Tettenhorst,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.08318},
already give a Shapley least-squares/Hodge treatment.
The recorded pooled-LaTeX slices, lines 88--115 and 561--581,
project marginal-contribution fields on the coalition hypercube.
The current projection instead fits restricted-game pair gaps with
a fixed skew matrix on agent identities.
The difference in objects does not itself establish novelty.
\end{itemize}
\ledger{49, 50}

Saari's dictionary, the BT/Borda equivalence, classical dividend
calculus, and the glove and assignment-game antecedents remain
credited. Source attempts and searches are recorded in
\url{ada/iterate_41_general_games.md}, lines 17--29,
with logs
\url{ada/run_051_balanced_sources.log} and
\url{ada/run_053_balanced_direct_sources.log}.
No PDF extraction was used when pooled LaTeX was available.
The searches did not establish that the latest formulas are new.
\ledger{49, 50}

\section{Objections resolved and unresolved in the latest round}

\begin{objection}
The general projection proves a universal failure of P's BT credit.
\end{objection}

It does not. It optimizes calibrated \emph{linear win-gap} error over
an unrestricted skew matrix under common-rate independent participation.
BT order agrees with win order, but BT log-score gaps do not equal
win gaps. Cardinal residuals do not automatically yield ordinal
lower bounds. Actual inputs may also reveal coalition context, violating
the fixed-comparator premise. \ledger{43, 50}

\begin{objection}
Higher dividend order alone determines the necessary observation order.
\end{objection}

It does not. The explicit \(d+1\) construction is a sufficient
worst-case bound for a defined additive zero-sum protocol and is
worst-case sharp when ties must be preserved.
The strict-only metric has different requirements.
Glove ordering has a linear threshold description yet violates binary
skew compatibility; a ternary rule resolves that violation.
Conversely, symmetric pair-dividend attribution can solve a
two-additive game without expressing it through pair wins.
\ledger{39, 46, 50}

\begin{objection}
The dividend repair is a practical replacement for P.
\end{objection}

No such result was obtained. Dividends are additional information,
and direct attribution is cheaper when exact coalition values are
already available. Whether visual local queries estimate the needed
blocks accurately and economically is unmeasured.
The construction answers a representation question. \ledger{46, 50}

\begin{objection}
The Bernoulli calculation gives the exact optimal error.
\end{objection}

Only for the four-agent case is attainment established for every
interior participation rate. The general expression averages valid
swapped-stratum lower bounds. Their simultaneous attainment, and the
asymptotic optimum, remain unresolved. No independent battery-process
assumption is attributed to P. \ledger{47, 50}

\section{What remains open}

The record now supports a general mathematical account in addition
to the selected market fixtures. A fixed pair-win model has coupled
membership coefficients; an arbitrary-game cardinal projection
separates higher-order dependence from incompatible low-order terms.
A dividend-plus-witness construction supplies exact ordinal recovery
for bounded-order games in a specified richer protocol.
The sharp dropout theorem supplies a strict-ranking stress test.
The favorable shared Q-grid fit prevents interpreting these statements
as broad failure of pair-local aggregation. \ledger{43, 46, 47, 50}

The material remains empirically thin. No real judge, MARL training,
Q trajectory, or matching P implementation was measured.
The work does not establish that P's observations omit relevant
context, that its judgments form a fixed comparator, or that
semantic improvements would accelerate learning.
Neither paper's conditional theorem, reported learning curves,
or trading data is refuted. \ledger{14, 29, 50}

Exact novelty remains unresolved, especially against the unavailable
social-choice originals and existing cooperative-game approximation
work. General strict-only ordinal representability is still not
characterized. Fixed-scale probability-constrained fitting and
nonlinear BT cardinal projection remain distinct open problems.
Heterogeneous or correlated participation, minimal heterogeneous-market
context, and joint-stratum Bernoulli attainment are also unresolved.
The latest work does not replace the original conditional claims with
an acceptance claim. \ledger{41, 49, 50}

\section{The smallest next step}

For theoretical novelty, compare the explicit projection, local-block
construction, and dropout formula with the missing original
Borda-dictionary texts and specialist work on cooperative-game
approximation. The existing retrieval record is insufficient to settle
that question. \ledger{49, 50}

For relevance to P, the smallest discriminating experiment remains
the frozen four-dropout-triple audit once inference access exists.
Compare repetitions, a scarcity summary, and a third-agent observation
at matched call and token budgets, using the same judge and a specified
regularized BT estimator. Measure raw pair probabilities before fitted
orders, and include direct symbolic attribution.
This tests whether the fixed-input premise holds and whether the
constructive context repair produces the predicted separation.
\ledger{39, 50}

A successful frozen-state test would establish a semantic effect for
the measured judge and inputs. It would still require a later
implementation and training study to establish embodied relevance
or learning benefit. Until then, the supported outcome is a conditional
theory-and-benchmark research line, with exact derivations, reproducible
structural checks, and explicit limits. \ledger{50}

